ing",[113,705,706,707,710,711,136],{},"Metaclasses, ",[69,708,709],{},"__new__",", class decorators, ",[69,712,713],{},"__getattr__",[84,715,717],{"id":716},"part-v-production-engineering","Part V — Production Engineering",[89,719,720,730],{},[92,721,722],{},[95,723,724,726,728],{},[98,725,100],{},[98,727,103],{},[98,729,106],{},[108,731,732,752,781,801,827],{},[95,733,734,737,743],{},[113,735,736],{},"24",[113,738,739],{},[119,740,742],{"href":741},"\u002Fpython\u002F24-testing","Testing",[113,744,745,132,748,751],{},[69,746,747],{},"unittest",[69,749,750],{},"pytest",", fixtures, mocking, parametrization, coverage.",[95,753,754,757,763],{},[113,755,756],{},"25",[113,758,759],{},[119,760,762],{"href":761},"\u002Fpython\u002F25-packaging-and-virtual-environments","Packaging & Virtual Environments",[113,764,765,239,768,239,771,239,774,154,777,780],{},[69,766,767],{},"venv",[69,769,770],{},"pip",[69,772,773],{},"pyproject.toml",[69,775,776],{},"uv",[69,778,779],{},"poetry",", publishing to PyPI.",[95,782,783,786,792],{},[113,784,785],{},"26",[113,787,788],{},[119,789,791],{"href":790},"\u002Fpython\u002F26-performance-and-optimization","Performance & Optimization",[113,793,794,797,798,800],{},[69,795,796],{},"cProfile",", memory views, ",[69,799,492],{},", when to reach for C extensions.",[95,802,803,806,812],{},[113,804,805],{},"27",[113,807,808],{},[119,809,811],{"href":810},"\u002Fpython\u002F27-security","Security",[113,813,814,816,817,154,820,823,824,136],{},[69,815,585],{}," risks, ",[69,818,819],{},"eval",[69,821,822],{},"exec"," dangers, SQL injection, ",[69,825,826],{},"secrets",[95,828,829,832,838],{},[113,830,831],{},"28",[113,833,834],{},[119,835,837],{"href":836},"\u002Fpython\u002F28-exercises-and-projects","Exercises & Projects",[113,839,840],{},"Capstone projects from beginner to advanced.",[19,842,844],{"id":843},"learning-path-suggestions","Learning Path Suggestions",[84,846,848],{"id":847},"if-youre-new-to-programming","If you're new to programming",[15,850,851],{},"Read 01–09 slowly, running every example. Don't skip the Edge Cases sections — Python's forgiving syntax hides real mechanics (mutability, references, truthiness) that will confuse you later if skipped now. Do the beginner exercises in chapter 28 after chapter 09, then continue to Part II.",[84,853,855],{"id":854},"if-youre-coming-from-javascript","If you're coming from JavaScript",[15,857,858,859,239,862,864,865,154,868,871,872,874],{},"Skim 01–04 (similar shape, different keywords: ",[69,860,861],{},"elif",[69,863,166],{},", no ",[69,866,867],{},"var",[69,869,870],{},"let"," distinction — Python has no block scope). Pay close attention to 05 (default argument mutation is a Python-specific trap JS doesn't have), 07–08 (Python's list\u002Fdict semantics around copying and mutation differ from JS arrays\u002Fobjects), and 21–22 (the GIL means Python's concurrency story is fundamentally different from Node's single-threaded event loop). Don't assume ",[69,873,187],{}," behaves like JS — read 03 carefully.",[84,876,878],{"id":877},"if-youre-coming-from-a-statically-typed-language-java-c-go","If you're coming from a statically-typed language (Java, C#, Go)",[15,880,881,882,885,886,888],{},"Python's duck typing (02) and dynamic dispatch will feel unfamiliar at first — lean into it rather than fighting it with excessive ",[69,883,884],{},"isinstance"," checks. Read 20 (Type Hints) early to get static-analysis safety back via ",[69,887,653],{},". Read 13–15 for how Python does OOP differently (multiple inheritance via MRO, properties instead of getters\u002Fsetters, duck-typed protocols instead of explicit interfaces).",[84,890,892],{"id":891},"if-you-have-a-data-science-background-pandasnumpy-but-shaky-on-core-python","If you have a data science background (pandas\u002Fnumpy but shaky on \"core\" Python)",[15,894,895,896,154,898,900,901,904,905,908,909,912],{},"You likely know 02, 04, 07–08 already — skim them for gaps (slicing edge cases, the ",[69,897,184],{},[69,899,187],{}," distinction). Focus on 05, 09–11 (comprehensions and generators are everywhere in idiomatic pipeline code), 16 (proper exception handling instead of bare ",[69,902,903],{},"except:","), 19 (iterators\u002Fcontext managers explain why ",[69,906,907],{},"with open(...)"," and generators in ",[69,910,911],{},"pandas","\u002Fnumpy code work the way they do), and 26 (performance — vectorization vs Python loops, memory views).",[19,914,916],{"id":915},"companion-resources","Companion Resources",[63,918,919,928,936,962,970,978],{},[27,920,921,927],{},[119,922,926],{"href":923,"rel":924},"https:\u002F\u002Fdocs.python.org\u002F3\u002F",[925],"nofollow","Python Official Documentation"," — the source of truth.",[27,929,930,935],{},[119,931,934],{"href":932,"rel":933},"https:\u002F\u002Frealpython.com\u002F",[925],"Real Python"," — deep, practical tutorials.",[27,937,938,943,944,949,950,955,956,961],{},[119,939,942],{"href":940,"rel":941},"https:\u002F\u002Fpeps.python.org\u002F",[925],"PEP Index"," — language design decisions, especially ",[119,945,948],{"href":946,"rel":947},"https:\u002F\u002Fpeps.python.org\u002Fpep-0008\u002F",[925],"PEP 8"," (style), ",[119,951,954],{"href":952,"rel":953},"https:\u002F\u002Fpeps.python.org\u002Fpep-0020\u002F",[925],"PEP 20"," (Zen of Python), ",[119,957,960],{"href":958,"rel":959},"https:\u002F\u002Fpeps.python.org\u002Fpep-0484\u002F",[925],"PEP 484"," (type hints).",[27,963,964,969],{},[119,965,968],{"href":966,"rel":967},"https:\u002F\u002Fdocs.python-guide.org\u002F",[925],"The Hitchhiker's Guide to Python"," — best practices and structure.",[27,971,972,977],{},[119,973,976],{"href":974,"rel":975},"https:\u002F\u002Fgithub.com\u002Fpython\u002Fcpython",[925],"CPython source"," — read the implementation once you're comfortable.",[27,979,980,985,986,991],{},[119,981,984],{"href":982,"rel":983},"https:\u002F\u002Freddit.com\u002Fr\u002Fpython",[925],"r\u002FPython"," and ",[119,987,990],{"href":988,"rel":989},